et\u2019s pick **W6**, as it\u2019s slightly less central (W7-W8-W9 better than W6-W7-W8? No).\n\nOr **W8** \u2014 being closer to 9, maybe more likely to be wanted.\n\nBut no data.\n\nAlternatively, **B7**: you have B7, B8, B9 \u2014 full chow \u2014 do NOT discard any of them.\n\n**B9**: even though safe (last one), breaking a complete chow is bad.\n\nSame for B7, B8.\n\nSo **do not play B7, B8, B9**.\n\n**T3**: don\u2019t play \u2014 need for pair.\n\n**T4**: risky, and not useful.\n\n**W6/W8**: safe, redundant.\n\nSo only viable candidates: **W6, W8, T4**\n\nAmong these, **T4 is most dangerous**, so eliminate.\n\nBetween **W6 and W8**, no major difference.\n\nBut **W7 has 4 unshown** \u2014 unusually high. Maybe someone has a concealed pung? Or just stuck?\n\nBut if someone has W7s, they might want W6 or W8 for chow.\n\nBut with W5 gone, W6-W7-W8 is only possible high chow.\n\nStill, risk is low.\n\nPerhaps **keep W8**, discard **W6**, because W8 is higher \u2014 slightly more terminal-like, but negligible.\n\nActually, **discard W6** \u2014 it appeared earlier in discards.\n\nFinal decision: **Play W6**\n\nIt\u2019s safe, doesn\u2019t break any potential in-hand meld, keeps flexibility.\n\nReason: W6 is part of a weak sequence (with W5 gone, W6-W7-W8 difficult to complete), and discarding it poses minimal risk. Keeping W8 offers similar value with slightly better potential for pairing with W7/W9 draws. Other tiles like B7-B9 and T3-T3 are crucial for forming a chow and pair, while T4 is both hard to use and potentially dangerous.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
